---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

// V-709 — Sentry integration page.
//
// W226.A — accuracy pass. The previous revision documented a
// customer-configurable Sentry forwarding feature (DSN setting,
// dashboard integration, sample-rate, source-map upload field on
// sessions.start) that does NOT exist anywhere in the codebase:
// there is no `/integrations/sentry` endpoint, no DSN setting on
// the account row, no `source_map_url` or `release` field on
// CreateSessionRequest, and no `script.error` webhook event type.
//
// Sentry is used internally for Driftstack's own server-side error
// observability (apps/server/src/lib/sentry.ts). Customer-facing
// Sentry forwarding is not implemented today. This page now says so.
---

<BaseLayout title="Sentry integration — Driftstack" description="Where Sentry sits in Driftstack today, and how to wire your own Sentry-instrumented page-under-test until customer-facing forwarding ships.">
  <main class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-12 prose prose-slate dark:prose-invert">
    <h1>Sentry integration</h1>

    <div class="rounded-md border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50 p-4 not-prose">
      <p class="m-0 text-sm text-amber-900">
        <strong>Status:</strong> there is no customer-configurable
        Sentry forwarder on the Driftstack control plane today.
        The dashboard does not expose a Sentry DSN setting,
        <code class="font-mono">sessions.create</code> does not
        accept a Sentry-related field, and we do not fan events out
        to customer Sentry projects. The page below documents what
        Sentry does on our side, and how to instrument your
        page-under-test until customer-facing forwarding lands.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>Where Sentry sits today</h2>
    <p>
      Driftstack uses Sentry internally for server-side error
      observability (Fastify error-handler + structured Pino-bridged
      events). Customer payloads are scrubbed before send by the
      sensitive-key denylist in
      <code>apps/server/src/lib/sentry.ts</code>; raw API keys,
      session secrets, customer-supplied URLs, and webhook secrets
      never reach sentry.io. See
      <a href="/docs/data-residency#what-does-leave-the-eu">the
      data-residency doc</a> for the residency posture and the PII
      filter rules.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sentry is on the Driftstack subprocessor list, not on every
      customer's. Errors from <em>your</em> code running inside a
      Driftstack browser session do NOT flow into our Sentry
      project — they're scoped to the session and surfaced via
      <code>session.failed</code> webhook deliveries (see
      <a href="/docs/webhooks">/docs/webhooks</a>).
    </p>

    <h2>Until customer forwarding ships</h2>
    <p>
      If you want stack traces from inside the page running under
      Driftstack, the recommended pattern today is to instrument
      Sentry <em>inside</em> the page itself — exactly as you would
      for any browser-side service:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Mint a Sentry project on your side and copy its DSN.</li>
      <li>Inject the Sentry browser SDK into your page-under-test
        (script tag, bundler import, or an injected snippet via
        <code>POST /v1/sessions/&lt;id&gt;/interact</code>).</li>
      <li>Tag events with your Driftstack session id — read it
        from the session-create response and pass it to
        <code>Sentry.setTag('driftstack_session_id', …)</code> on
        page boot. That lets you drill back from a Sentry issue to
        the Driftstack live-view.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Because the Sentry SDK runs inside the browser session, every
      capability of Sentry (release tagging, source maps, breadcrumbs,
      user identity, traces) works the same as it would on a normal
      end-user browser visiting the page. No Driftstack-side
      configuration is required.
    </p>

    <h2>Roadmap: control-plane forwarding</h2>
    <p>
      A control-plane-driven Sentry forwarder (Driftstack-managed DSN
      + server-side fan-out of <code>session.failed</code> stack
      traces) is on the backlog. When it ships, it will land:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>A new <code>POST /v1/account/integrations/sentry</code>
        endpoint (and the matching dashboard form) to register a DSN
        + sample rate.</li>
      <li>A new <code>integration.sentry.degraded</code> webhook
        event when the Sentry endpoint starts returning non-2xx.</li>
      <li>An entry on the
        <a href="/changelog">API changelog</a> with the migration
        notes.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Subscribe to <a href="/docs/status-subscriptions">status-page
      subscriptions</a> or the changelog RSS for the ship
      notification; in the meantime, instrument the page-under-test
      directly.
    </p>

    <h2>Related</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/docs/webhooks">Webhooks</a> — the in-place
        mechanism for receiving <code>session.failed</code>
        notifications.</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/data-residency">Data residency</a> — what
        Driftstack sends to its own Sentry project + how it's
        scrubbed.</li>
      <li><a href="/docs/error-codes">Error codes</a> — the stable
        RFC 7807 type URIs you can route on.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Need help?</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about timing or what to build toward in the meantime:
      <a href="mailto:integrations@driftstack.dev">integrations@driftstack.dev</a>.
    </p>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
